# Grundlage

A small, zero-dependency library for building web components with tagged template literals.

## Installation

```bash
npm install grundlage
```

## Concepts

### Components as Generators

Components are defined using generator functions. The generator gives you explicit control over the lifecycle: setup runs synchronously up to the first yield, each yield mounts a view, and a function returned from the generator is stored as the cleanup callback. `render` returns a constructor — registration with `customElements.define` is left to the caller.

```javascript
import { render, html } from "grundlage";

const AsyncComponent = render(async function* (element) {
	yield html`<p>loading</p>`;

	try {
		const data = await fetch("...").then((response) => response.json());
		yield html`<p>name: ${data.name}, age: ${data.age}</p>`;
	} catch (error) {
		yield html`<p>error: ${error}</p>`;
	}

	return () => {
		console.log("cleanup");
	};
});

customElements.define("async-component", AsyncComponent);
```

#### Yielding a template vs. yielding a function

You can yield either an `html` template directly or a function that returns one. The two behave differently when `element.update()` is called:

- `yield html\`...\`` — mounts the template once. Subsequent `update()` calls re-run with the same captured expressions, so the view stays put. Use this for static views and loading states.
- `yield () => html\`...\`` — mounts the result and re-invokes the function on every `update()`, picking up fresh expression values. Use this for any view that should react to state changes.

### Templating

The `html` tagged template literal parses your markup once per template strings array and caches the result. Subsequent renders only update the dynamic parts.

```javascript
html`<div class="card ${isActive ? "active" : ""}">
	<h2>${title}</h2>
	<p>${description}</p>
</div>`;
```

Supported bindings:

- Content: `<p>${text}</p>`
- Attributes: `<div class=${className}>`
- Mixed attributes: `<div class="static ${dynamic}">`
- Event handlers: `<button onClick=${handler}>`
- Lists: `<ul>${items.map(i => html`<li>${i}</li>`)}</ul>`
- Nested templates: `<div>${showDetails ? html`<details>...</details>` : null}</div>`
- Dynamic tags: `<${tagName}>content</${tagName}>` — open and close must both be dynamic
- Raw content (`style`, `script`, `textarea`, `template`): `<style>${css}</style>`

### Props

Reading inputs on a web component is awkward: attributes are always strings, anything else has to be set as a JS property, and the two paths need different handling. The `props` helper takes the element and a schema of constructors, validates and coerces the input, and gives you a typed object regardless of how the value was passed in.

Each schema entry can take three forms:

- `Constructor` — required, throws if missing
- `[Constructor]` — optional, resolves to `undefined` if missing
- `[Constructor, defaultValue]` — optional with a fallback

```javascript
import { render, html, props } from "grundlage";

const PropsExample = render(function* (element) {
	const { label, count, disabled, callback, items, missing } = props(element, {
		label: String,
		count: Number,
		disabled: Boolean,
		callback: Function,
		items: Array,
		missing: [String, "default"],
	});

	yield () => html`<button onClick=${callback} disabled=${disabled}>${label} (${count})</button>`;
});

customElements.define("props-example", PropsExample);
```

### Reactivity

When you call `element.update()`, Grundlage diffs the new expression array against the previous one and only touches the DOM for entries that actually changed:

- Primitives are compared by reference (`===`).
- Objects, functions, and nested `html` templates are compared by a content hash, so structurally equal values don't trigger DOM work.
- Arrays always re-run their binding; per-item reuse happens inside the list reconciler via hash-based identity matching, so unchanged items keep their nodes.

```javascript
const TodoList = render(function* (element) {
	const items = [];

	const add = (text) => {
		items.push({ id: Date.now(), text });
		element.update();
	};

	yield () => html`
		<ul>
			${items.map((item) => html`<li>${item.text}</li>`)}
		</ul>
	`;
});

customElements.define("todo-list", TodoList);
```

### Server rendering

Components hydrate an existing shadow root if one is already attached when the constructor runs. On the hydration pass, tag, content, and raw-content bindings are trusted as-is from the server markup; only attribute bindings re-run, so event listeners and non-stringable JS properties get reattached. After the first `update()` the component switches to client-side rendering and behaves normally.
